> Results

```
=== max of x e^{-x} on [0,5] ===
x* = 1 (true 1), f(x*) = 0.367879 (true 1/e = 0.367879), iterations = 56

min of (x-3)^2+1: x* = 3 (true 3), f = 1 (true 1)

bracket width reached 1e-9 in 56 steps
wrote ternary_search.svg
```

Both optima land on their analytic values \-\- the peak of \(x e^{-x}\) at
\(x=1\) with value \(1/e\), and the parabola's minimum at \(x=3\) \-\- found
with nothing but function evaluations. The bracket-width trace is a straight line on a
log scale: geometric \((2/3)^t\) shrinkage, the signature of logarithmic
convergence.


The purple actual-width line lies right on top of the grey \((2/3)^t\) reference:
every step really does discard one third of the bracket. That steady logarithmic
descent is why ternary search reaches a \(10^{-9}\) bracket in only a few dozen
evaluations, no derivative required \-\- as long as the function truly has a single peak.
